## The Floor Scrapers: why were these workers 'vulgar'?
sources:
    - id: orsay
      title: Raboteurs de parquets (RF 2718)
      authors: Musée d'Orsay, Paris
      year: 2024
      finding: '1875, oil on canvas, 102 × 147 cm, gift 1894; one of the first pictures of the urban working class; Bonnat''s training, drawn part by part and squared up; torsos like ancient heroes; refused by the 1875 Salon jury, probably shocked by its raw realism (some critics spoke of a ''vulgar subject''); shown at the second Impressionist exhibition, 1876; Zola: ''bourgeois painting through exactness'''
      url: https://www.musee-orsay.fr/fr/oeuvres/raboteurs-de-parquets-105
    - id: wiki
      title: Les raboteurs de parquet
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Rejected at the Salon of 1875; critics called the subject vulgar; shown at the 1876 Impressionist exhibition; given by the family in 1894
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_raboteurs_de_parquet

    card title "The Floor Scrapers"

Three men on their knees, scraping a floor.
    image art cites ["orsay"] credit "Caillebotte, 1875. Musée d'Orsay, Paris. Public domain." x 500 y 340 width 880 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gustave_Caillebotte_-_The_Floor_Planers_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Gustave_Caillebotte_-_The_Floor_Planers_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.boards [0.45,0.26,0.4,0.25] regions.clue [0.17,0,0.83,0.26] regions.floor [0,0.24,1,0.76] regions.hammer [0.595,0.575,0.09,0.09] regions.left_back [0.05,0.22,0.13,0.13] regions.left_hands [0.16,0.53,0.085,0.07] regions.left_man [0.005,0.215,0.225,0.39] regions.light_floor [0.02,0.3,0.42,0.65] regions.men [0,0.21,0.85,0.57] regions.mid_back [0.41,0.35,0.15,0.11] regions.mid_hands [0.39,0.66,0.075,0.11] regions.middle_man [0.385,0.33,0.21,0.44] regions.pair [0.385,0.32,0.455,0.46] regions.panels [0.43,0,0.57,0.26] regions.right_back [0.69,0.33,0.125,0.13] regions.right_hands [0.725,0.64,0.065,0.1] regions.right_man [0.675,0.325,0.165,0.42] regions.shavings [0.245,0.525,0.165,0.085] regions.signature [0.73,0.925,0.19,0.075] regions.window [0.17,0,0.255,0.22] regions.wine [0.915,0.515,0.075,0.215]

    camera on ["art@[0.58,0.7,0.42,0.3]"]

In 1875, (@highlight art:signature effect ring@) this picture was turned away from the biggest art show in Paris. Why?

    camera reset true

It is The Floor Scrapers, by Gustave Caillebotte, a well-off young Parisian, still in his twenties.
    portrait caillebotte of "Gustave Caillebotte" cameo true

    erase caillebotte

    camera on ["art:mid_hands"] zoom 3

First, what are the men doing? Each o (@highlight art:mid_hands@)ne pulls a blade along the boards, shaving a new wooden floor smooth.

    camera on ["art@[0.2,0.45,0.5,0.3]"]

The shavings lie everywhere in pale curls,
    highlight art:shavings

and a hammer waits by the middle man's knee.
    highlight art:hammer

    camera reset true

And how do we see them? From above, as if we stood in the room, looking down over their backs.
    highlight art:men strength soft

The boards run in straight lines toward the window, pulling the eye deep into the room: textbook perspective.
    highlight art:boards

Light from that window
    highlight art:window

slides across the floor and catches every stripe and every arm.
    highlight art:light_floor

    ask "Tap the clue." spot clue on art
        intro: One thing in this room says it isn't the workers' home.
        right: Yes: the iron balcony and the gilded walls.
        wrong: Look past the men, to the far end of the room.

    camera on ["art@[0.15,0.0,0.85,0.3]"]

A balcony of wrought iron, walls with gilded mouldings: a smart Paris apartment, being made ready for someone else.
    highlight art:clue lift true

    camera reset true

Look at the bodies: bare backs and shoulders, modelled like the statues art students copied.
    highlight art:men

    camera on ["art:mid_back"] zoom 3

In the art schools, the nude male body belonged to heroes of ancient myth. Here it belongs to three Paris workmen.
    focus art:mid_back rest grey

    camera on ["art:window"]

And they are city workers. Peasants, like Millet's gleaners, (@highlight art:window@) had been painted before; workers in the city almost never.

So why did the Salon turn it away?
    camera reset true

    pause 1

    camera on ["art@[0.25,0.22,0.7,0.62]"]

The Salon was the official yearly exhibition. Its jury refused the picture, probably shocked by its raw realism.
    focus art:pair strength strong rest grey

    camera reset true

Some critics called it a 'vulgar subject'.
    highlight art:men lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.2,0.3,0.5,0.45]"]

Vulgar meant low and common:  (@highlight art:shavings@)sweat and shavings, painted at the size and polish meant for heroes.

    camera reset true

Caillebotte took it to the Impressionists instead, and in 1876 it hung in their second exhibition.
    highlight art:floor strength soft

Even there, Émile Zola admired it but called it bourgeois painting, because it was so exact.
    portrait zola x 800 y 560 width 150 of "Émile Zola" cameo true

    erase zola

    camera on ["art@[0.3,0.25,0.62,0.6]"]

So the vulgarity was not in the men. It was in the match: the Academy's own tools spent on a job nobody thought worth a picture.
    highlight art:pair rest paper lift true

    pause 1

    camera reset true

    quiz "A painter today wants to cause the same stir Caillebotte did. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: The polished rider: an everyday worker given the size and care once kept for generals and heroes.
        wrong: Not the worker alone, nor a message: the grandeur he was given.
        * A quick pencil sketch of a delivery rider
        + A huge, polished painting of a delivery rider at work
        * A huge, polished portrait of a famous general
        * A delivery rider painted beside a protest slogan

His family gave it to the French state in 1894, the year he died, and it now hangs in the Musée d'Orsay. The floor got its picture after all.
    highlight art:floor lift true

